# fast-ssim2 0.7.1 vs 0.8.2 — per-cell score equivalence
#
# Question: can jxl-encoder's `fast-ssim2 = "0.7.1"` pin (jxl-encoder/Cargo.toml
# :65 dep, :415 dev-dep) be bumped to 0.8.2 without moving encoder output?
# `fast-ssim2` feeds `ssim2-loop`, which drives encode decisions, so a score
# shift moves bytes and re-bakes hash locks.
#
# ANSWER: NO. 0 of 84 comparable cells are bit-identical. DO NOT BUMP without
# an owner decision. The pin stays at 0.7.1.
#
# date            2026-08-31
# commit          (this commit)
# host            Darwin 25.5.0, aarch64 (Apple Silicon)
# harness         scripts/ssim2_version_equivalence/  (cargo run --release)
# corpus          ~/work/codec-corpus  CID22-512 validation x2, gb82-sc x2,
#                 plus 64x64 / 33x17 / 9x9 / 5x5 crops of a CID22 image
# grid            8 sources x 3 degradation families (block-quant / box-blur /
#                 additive-noise) x 4 strengths = 96 cells
# api exercised   `Ssimulacra2Reference::new(src).compare(dist)` — exactly the
#                 call `vardct/ssim2_loop.rs:172,327` makes on the encode path
#
# METHOD NOTE — why this is trustworthy:
#  * BOTH versions are linked into ONE binary via renamed deps
#    (`ssim_old = { package = "fast-ssim2", version = "=0.7.1" }`, `ssim_new`
#    = "=0.8.2"), so every cell is scored by both on the SAME input bytes in
#    the SAME process. No cross-run or cross-build drift can leak in.
#  * DETERMINISM CONTROL: the whole binary was run twice and the two outputs
#    are byte-identical, so the divergence is a VERSION delta, not noise.
#  * Reported PER CELL, never aggregated — an aggregate mean hides a real
#    shift, which is how the dssim-core 3.5.1 change nearly slipped through
#    (all 216 scores moved while the mean moved in the seventh decimal).
#
# RESULT
#   cells scored by both      84
#     bit-identical            0
#     different               84   (100%)
#   sub-8px behaviour change  12   (0.7.1 = Err(InvalidImageSize), 0.8.2 scores)
#
#   max ABS delta  4.156e-1 points on the 0-100 scale
#                  gb82_graph 796x481 quant/3: 79.54576145 -> 79.96140382
#   max REL delta  5.225e-3  (same cell)
#
# WHY, per the fast-ssim2 CHANGELOG (~/work/zen/fast-ssim2/CHANGELOG.md):
#  * 0.8.1 "Skip per-channel SSIM and edge-difference work whose final-score
#    weight is zero. Bit-identical to the prior path"
#  * 0.8.2 "Vectorised the recursive-Gaussian blur horizontal pass ...
#    Bit-identical output to the scalar path ... x86 (AVX2) unaffected"
#  Both claim bit-identity, and BOTH claims are about an individual internal
#  change, NOT about the composite 0.7.1 -> 0.8.2 delta. Note the 0.8.2 blur
#  change explicitly does NOT affect x86 — it changes the ARM path, which is
#  this host. A measurement on an x86 box may well disagree with this table;
#  that is a reason to re-measure per platform, not to trust either result
#  as universal.
#  * 0.8.2 also added sub-8px reflect-padding, turning InvalidImageSize into a
#    score. That is the 12 behaviour-change cells and is documented, intended,
#    and unreachable from `ssim2_loop` only if the loop never sees a <8px
#    image -- NOT verified here.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY
#  * It does not say the 0.8.2 scores are WORSE. They may well be more correct
#    (the zero-weight skip and the IIR vectorisation are both principled).
#    It says only that they are DIFFERENT, so the bump is a byte-moving change
#    that needs a lock re-bake and an owner decision, not a routine bump.
#  * The distortions are synthetic (quant/blur/noise on real images), not jxl
#    reconstructions. That is fine for a metric-equivalence question — the
#    metric is a pure function of two images — but a bump decision may want
#    real reconstructions across the encoder's actual operating range.
